in quantum mechanics we use a wave  function to describe everything there is  to know about a particle for example its  distribution and space the wave function  describes the wave nature of particles  squaring the wave function gives a  probability density or the likelihood of  finding the particles at any given point  a probability of zero would mean there  is no chance of the event happening and  a probability of one would mean it's  certain because the probability is  spread out over space the probability of  finding a particle at each point is  going to be a value between zero and one  if you add up all the probabilities what  you're doing is integrating the function  and the sum will be one because it is  certain that we will find the particles  somewhere along our distribution the  size starter is the complex conjugate of  the wave function and this whole term is  used to essentially square the wave  function but it is done this way because  the wave function might be a complex  number or contain the number I integral  quantities are common in quantum  mechanics you often want to find the sum  of probabilities over a certain range  and you can write it in a simplified way  this is Dirac notation on the left this  is the conjugate of our wave function  and on the right is our state this is  the state that the particle or whatever  else we're describing isn't if there are  multiple states which a particle can  exist in then we can write a mini  superposition of states  a and B that coefficients are the  amplitude of our wave function and  squaring them gives the probability of  that state Schrodinger's cat is a  thought experiment where the cat exists  in a quantum superposition of states  where the states are either alive or  dead because the cat's fate is linked to  the random decay of a radioactive  particle the coefficients in this case 1  over root 2 the same since there is  equal chance of the cat being either  alive or dead  squaring gives a value of 1/2 so if each  state has a probability of 1/2 these add  up to 1  as expected the cat has to be either  alive or dead it's certainly going to be  one of the two and there's no  probability of being otherwise
 

